{:ts "2026-07-28T00:00:00Z", :kind :decision, :origin "rheos-event-store-git-worldline-adr", :owner "claude-code", :dod "Event-store candidates in PR #158 reviewed; one candidate selected to receive the local worktree/branch-switch spike", :pi "rheos", :host "local", :manifest ["docs/notes/design/rheos-ledger-authority-and-branch-projections.md" "kanban/tasks/rheos-event-store-git-worldline-adr.md"], :refs "PR #158 head 9fe5787; seam at packages/rheos/src/rheos/backend/infra/ledger.cljs get-ledger", :tests "None run. This turn is review and recording only; no Rheos code changed and no spike executed.", :decisions "Spike goes to repository-local state under the Git directory plus dedicated refs. Git already supplies two-tier scoping without a synthesized identity scheme: --git-common-dir is shared across worktrees (committed segments, repo identity) and --git-dir is per worktree (pending events), so worktree isolation is structural and `git worktree remove` reclaims pending state. Nothing under the Git directory is rewritten by checkout, so the primary acceptance criterion holds by construction; refs/eta-mu/events/* are real refs so objects survive gc and clone reconstruction is a fetch refspec. External repo-identity-keyed state rejected as active store: Git exposes no stable repository UUID and a synthesized one is ambiguous under fork, remote rename, and multiple clones on one machine. Git notes rejected as active store, retained as optional attribution index: notes attach to commits so pending events cannot be represented, refs/notes/* is not fetched or pushed by default, and rebase drops notes unless notes.rewriteRef is set.", :note "Empirical basis observed in this repository: .git/worktrees/pr157 carries its own HEAD, index, and logs, and .git/opencode is existing precedent for tool state inside the Git directory. The ADR remains the accepting authority; this review only selects the spike target. Known costs recorded: a fetch/push refspec convention must be documented, and Git-directory state is invisible to ordinary code review."}
